  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div id=\"primary\"> <article id=\"post-1511616\"><section><div id=\"\"><div><nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumbs\"><ol><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/\"><span>Science</span></a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-index/\"><span>APOD</span></a></li><li aria-current=\"page\"><span>APOD: 2026 November 16 – NGC...</span></li></ol></nav></div><div><nav><ul><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/\">Today's APOD</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/submit/\">Submissions</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-index/\">Index</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-search/\">Search</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-calendar/\">Calendar</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-education/\">Education</a></li><li><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/apod-about/\">About</a></li><li><a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261116\">Discuss</a></li></ul></nav></div></div><div>\n\n<div id=\"\"> <p>APOD</p> <p>Astronomy Picture of the Day</p> <p> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer. </p> <div id=\"\"></div> <div> <div> <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> How do stars form? To help answer this question, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> used the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">James Webb Space Telescope</a> to take the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/\">featured detailed image</a> of the star-forming region <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NGC_7129\">NGC 7129</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/07_infraredwaves/\">infrared light</a>. Star formation progresses when <a href=\"https://www.labxchange.org/library/items/lb:LabXchange:bc30bab5:lx_simulation:1\">gravity</a> pulls together massive amounts of gas, but soon things can get complicated when previously formed stars emit powerful <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-july-28-young-stars-stellar-jets/\">jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-february-3-wolf-rayet-star-124-stellar-wind-machine/\">winds</a> -- and some then <a href=\"https://youtu.be/-yEBVm5o97E\">explode</a>. Opaque <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/dust-in-the-stellar-wind-a-cosmological-primer/\">dust</a> can form in the cool <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/sci/meetings/2024/Bthelines/Ludwig.pdf\">atmospheres of stars</a> and, when dispersed, can obstruct <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible-light</a> images. Many stars can be seen to be forming in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/ngc-7129-nircam-image/#:~:text=Interactive:\">featured image</a>, with the young stars on the left, in the gold-colored gas, typically being more mature than the young stars on the right -- in the red region. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-may-28-herbig-haro-24/\">Jets</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2000-march-18-a-wind-from-the-sun/\">wind</a>s likely cause the filamentary nature of the red-colored gas. Studying stars at different stages of development <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734167121479-6147220c23f0?q=80&amp;w=687&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;ixid=NHwxMjA3fDA%3D\">bolsters understanding</a> about what <a href=\"https://scixplorer.org/abs/2020SSRv..216...68G/abstract\">physical processes</a> are most important and when.</p><p><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> sky duel </p></div> <div> <table> <tbody> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Date:</th> <td> November 16, 2026 </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Credit:</th> <td> <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.esa.int/\">ESA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.asc-csa.gc.ca/eng/\">CSA</a>, <a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">JWST</a>; <br>Processing: <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/alyssa-pagan-058170144\">A. Pagan</a> (<a href=\"https://www.stsci.edu/\">STScI</a>) </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">Authors &amp; editors:</th> <td> Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe </td> </tr> <tr> <th scope=\"row\">A service of:</th> <td> <a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/astrophysics/\" target=\"_blank\">ASD</a> at <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\" target=\"_blank\">GSFC</a>,<br><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/about-science-activation/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA Science Activation</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/\" target=\"_blank\">Michigan Tech. U.</a> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div> </div> </div>\n\n<p id=\"\"> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/random-apod/\" target=\"_self\" aria-label=\"Random APOD Generator\"> <span>Random APOD Generator</span> <svg viewBox=\"0 0 32 32\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"16\" r=\"16\"></circle><path d=\"M8 16.956h12.604l-3.844 4.106 1.252 1.338L24 16l-5.988-6.4-1.252 1.338 3.844 4.106H8v1.912z\"></path></svg> </a> </p> <div id=\"\"> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-15-leonid-meteors-through-orion/\"> <div> <p> Yesterday's Image </p> <p> APOD: 2026 November 15 – Leonid Meteors Through Orion </p> </div> </a> <a href=\"#\"> <div> <p> Tomorrow's Image </p> </div> </a> </div></div></section></article>\n</div></div>",
